  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1211 PM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nCurrent conditions - Mid Week:\n\nSurface high pressure resides over the Northland leading to clear \nskies and and light winds across the region. Temperatures this \nafternoon will warm into the low 40s. Upper level ridging is firmly \nset up across the Northern Plains and is expected to linger through \nthe middle of the upcoming work week. The coldest portion of the air \nmass will gradually shift east through this period and allow warmer \nair to filter in from the south and west. Temperatures will continue \ntheir gradual climb to well above normal. Highs will ascend into the \n50s by Wednesday which is 10-15F above normal for this time of year. \nWe may also end up breaking some warm low temperatures on Wednesday \nand Thursday with early morning temperatures currently forecasted to \nbe in the upper 30s to low 40s. \n\nLate Week - Weekend:\n\nA pattern shift is expected to occur late Wednesday as troughing \npushes in from the west and nudges the ridge out of the region. This \nwill open the door for a more active weather pattern with models \nhighlighting two potential systems that could impact the Northland. \nThe first system is projected to move across the Northern Plains and \njog north towards Ontario. The exact path remains nebulous this far \nout but given the synoptic set up the system will have to contend \nwith a fair amount of dry air as it approaches. Warm temps in place \nwill also lend itself to rain being the dominant precipitation type.\n\nEarly indications from available ensembles and deterministic guidance \nsuggest that the second system that arrives next weekend could be \nmore impactful. While exact track remains uncertain the models do \nshow some agreement with the system being stronger as pressure \nvalues range from 990-1000mb. Nothing extreme but more in line with \nour typical Colorado Low set up. Which would also lead to higher \nPWATs to produce better precipitation rates. Depending on the track \nwe could introduce some snow on the backside of this system as cold \nair will likely be waiting over the Canadian Prairies.",
